User-visible changes in jlib.  The full history is in git; this is the summary
a packager or a returning user wants.


2.0.0 (unreleased)
------------------

Compatibility
    This release is compatible with nothing before it, which is what the
    major bump says.  Three things break: the licence changed, C++20 became
    mandatory where it was not, and libsigc++ left the public headers in
    favour of std:: and jlib/sys/signal.hh.

    Headers install under $(includedir)/jlib-2.0/ and the pkg-config file is
    jlib-2.0.pc, so 1.2 and 2.0 can be installed side by side.

    libj_so_version is 7:0:0.  It had read 6:6:1 since the 2011 CVS import
    and through every change above, so the soname had been claiming a
    compatibility that stopped being true some years ago.

License
    Relicensed from GNU GPL v2-or-later to Apache License 2.0.  Everything up
    to and including the relicensing commit remains available under the old
    terms.  It became possible only when the last file jlib did not hold the
    copyright to -- a third-party XML parser -- was replaced clean-room.

Build
    C++20 is now required and probed for explicitly.  Builds on macOS (Apple
    clang, arm64) and modern Linux (gcc 13).  Out-of-tree builds expected.
    libsigc++ and glibmm are gone, replaced by std:: and jlib/sys/signal.hh.
    OSS audio replaced by PortAudio; GLUT replaced by GLFW.

Parsing
    A new ABNF parser-combinator core, jlib/util/abnf.hh, with an RFC 5234
    text front end -- an RFC's grammar can be pasted into a header and read as
    it stands.  Addresses (RFC 5322), MIME headers (2045/2047/2231), URLs
    (3986), dates, IMAP responses (3501), HTTP messages (9110/9112) and XML
    are all read this way now rather than by find() and substr().

Mail
    OAuth2: the refresh grant, the authorization-code grant with PKCE, a
    loopback redirect receiver, and XOAUTH2 for IMAP.  Gmail has required this
    since 2022 and Outlook.com for personal accounts since September 2024, so
    a client with only LOGIN and AUTH PLAIN could reach neither.

Servers
    jlib::sys::server accepts a connection and hands it to a handler as a
    stream, plain or TLS, dispatching through a job_queue.  jlib::net::http::
    server reads a request against the same RFC 9112 grammar the client's
    responses go through, and can stream a response for server-sent events.
    Both are for loopback and a test harness, and both say so in their headers.

Machine learning
    A new jlib/ai module runs quantized transformers from GGUF files: llama,
    qwen2 and gemma2 architectures, SentencePiece and GPT-2 byte-level
    vocabularies, f32/f16/q8_0/q4_K/q6_K tensors, with a Metal compute backend
    where there is one.  Chat templates are rendered by evaluating the model's
    own Jinja2 template rather than by scanning it.

    New programs: jchat, jalpaca (a curses front end), and jserve -- an
    OpenAI-compatible endpoint, which aider drives end to end.

Graphics
    The headline goal since 1999 is done: jhardhyper draws 4-D objects as
    solid, depth-sorted, translucent 3-D geometry, and under stereographic
    projection shows the Hopf foliation as nested tori.


1.2.0
-----
    New directory layout enabling vpath builds and an internal make check.
